{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Keep the installed curl package at its current version so a system update leaves it unchanged.","status":"satisfied","summary":"curl was initially installed at 8.17.0-r1, while an unpinned upgrade simulation selected 8.22.0-r0. The executor added an exact 8.17.0-r1 world constraint, and subsequent pre- and post-restart system-upgrade simulations excluded both curl and libcurl. The installed version and constraint persisted across restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d9732b06b335488f","cmd-d4561e5729914813","cmd-7d38b2f01cbc4e3c","cmd-ac66272d29de4344","cmd-f93d17dcd75e4c47"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Preserve the installed operating-system release and kernel, using an approach compatible with the legacy Alpine platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The system remained Alpine 3.23.3 with running kernel 6.18.7-0-lts before configuration, after configuration, and after restart. The implementation used Alpine's native apk world constraint and installed no replacement platform software.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4582f87f334a474b","cmd-ac66272d29de4344","cmd-2b3d64b852f94a1b","cmd-f93d17dcd75e4c47","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.96,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.91,"summary":"No causally unnecessary managed-system mutation was identified: refreshing indexes supported upgrade comparison, editing /etc/apk/world implemented the hold, and restarting fulfilled the mandatory evidence protocol. The failed first edit left /etc/apk/world.new, but the corrective command validated and promoted that same file, so it contributed to the final solution rather than being an unrelated experiment (cmd-9a687ca5df064f0b, cmd-7d38b2f01cbc4e3c). Hygiene is reduced because three executor-created evidence/restart files remained in /tmp: apk-upgrade-sim.out, apk-upgrade-post-restart.out, and benchmark-reboot.log. They were bounded and low risk but not cleaned up (cmd-ac66272d29de4344, cmd-953645af4206407d, cmd-f93d17dcd75e4c47). No new listener, package inconsistency, failed service, or platform change was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3e7e379a657b4dc7","cmd-9a687ca5df064f0b","cmd-7d38b2f01cbc4e3c","cmd-ac66272d29de4344","cmd-953645af4206407d","cmd-f93d17dcd75e4c47","global:before_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied. The curl hold is directly supported by before-and-after upgrade simulations with a newer curl available, and it persisted through a verified node restart. The OS release and running kernel remained unchanged. The provider-wide restart evidence is strong: it includes pre-restart identity and required state, acknowledgement, observed connection disruption, a different post-restart boot ID, recovered package/configuration state, running base services, and successful curl connectivity; lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the transition.","limitations":["The upgrade behavior was tested with apk's simulation rather than by applying all 58 planned package upgrades. This was a reasonable bounded test because a real upgrade would have changed platform packages the task explicitly required preserving, but apk warned that the simulation might be unreliable while a pre-upgrade was available (cmd-ac66272d29de4344, cmd-f93d17dcd75e4c47).","The restart protocol did not capture explicit pre-restart OpenRC status for networking and chronyd. It did capture the required package/configuration state and readable root filesystem before restart, baseline networking sockets, and direct post-restart service and connectivity checks (cmd-2b3d64b852f94a1b, cmd-f93d17dcd75e4c47, global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets)."]}
